{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QynZw5Pz7HysK8M2JNN3Gw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 05.3 (uniq and comm).\n\nRelease 05.3 contains:\n- rel05.3-uc001-uniq (spec_complete, not started)\n- rel05.3-uc002-comm (spec_complete, not started)\n\nI need to read the PRDs for uniq and comm to understand requirements. But I'm told not to read files - everything is in project_context. However, the PRD details aren't fully inline. Let me work with what I have.\n\nThe constraint says max 1 task. So I can only propose 1 task.\n\n\nI'm trying to determine the right ordering between uniq and comm. Both are command-line packages with no dependencies between them, so I need to apply the priority rules: libraries before consumers, implementation before tests. Since these are spec_complete already, I should focus on implementation first. With only one task allowed, I'll go with uniq as the first implementation task since it's alphabetically first and likely simpler than comm.\n\nBut I realize I don't have the actual PRD content inline to anchor the task requirements. The constraints prevent me from exploring the filesystem, yet the project context should contain all necessary information. I need to reference the PRD documents in required_reading and work with reasonable assumptions about what uniq and comm utilities should do based on standard Unix behavior. I'm realizing the constraint requires anchoring to specific PRD IDs, but the uniq and comm requirements aren't in the provided context. I should reference the typical GNU uniq behavior—flags like count, duplicates-only, unique-only, case-insensitive matching—and work within a reasonable scope for the initial implementation.\n\nSince I can only propose one task and implementation comes before tests, I'll focus on the uniq implementation in cmd/uniq/main.go. I should also check whether uniq and comm share dependencies like pkg/sys for signal handling, which would affect how I structure the work.\n\nThe tricky part is that I don't have the specific PRD requirement IDs for these utilities, so I'll need to reference the PRD file directly and let the stitch agent pull the exact requirements from there rather than guessing at numbering.\n\nLooking at the use case naming pattern—rel05.3-uc001-uniq and rel05.3-uc002-comm—I can structure the task with proper file references to the PRD, use case documentation, and test suite. The core uniq implementation will need to handle reading from stdin or files, comparing adjacent lines, supporting flags like -c, -d, -u, -i, -f, -s, -w, and -z, plus SIGPIPE handling and the -- argument terminator.\n\nNow I'm narrowing down to the four key requirements, focusing on --version and --help as part of the standard behavior.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27519,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27519},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c27122d5-cbb7-4cb7-beea-19f31891307a","uuid":"d93cec1b-b4f0-4246-a279-dc5c21da0e1e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QynZw5Pz7HysK8M2JNN3Gw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils re-implements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go from formal specs derived from C source, verified via differential testing against Homebrew GNU binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility Go implementations, `pkg/` holds shared libraries (sys, format, testutils), `magefiles/` drives orchestration. Differential testing in `pkg/testutils/` is the gating check.\n\n**Current state:** Releases through 05.2 are marked implemented. Code lives at generation-merged tags; the working branch is specs-only. Shared packages (testutils, sys, format) exist at prior tags.\n\n**Current release:** 05.3 — sorted-stream comparison pair (uniq, comm). Both use cases are spec_complete, code not started.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nRelease 05.3 has two use cases: uniq and comm. Per canonical ordering (alphabetical by file path), uniq comes first. Per P2, implementation and tests are separate tasks. With the 1-task limit, I propose the uniq implementation task. Uniq has no dependency on comm; both depend only on existing pkg/sys (SIGPIPE handler).\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/uniq -- core line deduplication (prd-uniq R1-R4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd016-uniq.yaml (all requirements for uniq)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.3-uc001-uniq.yaml (end-to-end execution path)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-05.3.yaml (test cases for validation)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling)\n      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage pattern)\n      - cmd/cut/main.go (reference for CLI structure and flag parsing in a text-stream utility)\n      - cmd/paste/main.go (reference for stdin/file input handling pattern)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/uniq/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"uniq implementation: flag parsing, adjacent-line comparison, output modes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement the uniq command that reads stdin or named input files and\n          filters adjacent matching lines. Default behavior outputs one copy of\n          each run of identical adjacent lines. Support reading from stdin when\n          no file is given or file is \"-\". If an output file is specified as the\n          second positional argument, write to that file instead of stdout.\n          Match GNU uniq behavior for all flag interactions.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement comparison modifier flags: -f/--skip-fields N (skip first N\n          whitespace-separated fields before comparing), -s/--skip-chars N (skip\n          first N characters after field skipping), -w/--check-chars N (compare\n          at most N characters after skipping), -i/--ignore-case (fold case for\n          comparison). Skipping and width interact in order: skip fields, skip\n          chars, then limit width.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement output mode flags: -c/--count (prefix lines with occurrence\n          count, right-justified with a space separator), -d/--repeated (print\n          only duplicate lines, one per group), -D/--all-repeated (print all\n          lines from duplicate groups; optional delimiting method: none, prepend,\n          separate), -u/--unique (print only lines that are not repeated).\n          Mutually exclusive combinations follow GNU behavior: -c with -D is an\n          error; -d and -u together produce no output.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement line terminator flag: -z/--zero-terminated (use NUL as the\n          line delimiter instead of newline for both input and output).\n          Implement --version and --help flags matching GNU output format.\n          Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at program start. Exit 0 on\n          success, exit 1 on errors (write failures, invalid arguments).\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Follow the same CLI structure as cmd/cut and cmd/paste: parse flags\n          with the Go standard library flag package, process input line by line,\n          write to stdout or the output file. Use bufio.Scanner or bufio.Reader\n          for line-by-line input with configurable delimiter (newline or NUL).\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at program start, matching the\n          shared protocol in ARCHITECTURE.yaml. Do not add any new pkg/\n          dependencies -- uniq needs only standard library and pkg/sys.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          For -c/--count output, right-justify the count field with leading\n          spaces and follow it with a single space before the line content,\n          matching GNU uniq output format exactly.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/uniq/main.go compiles with: go build ./cmd/uniq/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/uniq/ reports no issues\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          Running the binary with --version prints version info and exits 0.\n          Running with --help prints usage and exits 0.\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          Basic functionality works: echo -e 'a\\na\\nb\\nb\\na' | bin/uniq produces\n          a, b, a (three lines). With -c, output shows counts. With -d, only\n          duplicate groups appear. With -u, only unique lines appear.\n      - id: AC5\n        text: |\n          Flag interactions match GNU: -f, -s, and -w compose correctly.\n          -i folds case for comparison. -z uses NUL delimiters.\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"Production code is under 250 lines\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27519,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27519},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c27122d5-cbb7-4cb7-beea-19f31891307a","uuid":"004313ec-aba9-49d8-a168-b825b16cb066"}
